<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sonnō jōi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sonnō jōi, meaning revere the emperor, expel the barbarians, was a political slogan of the late Tokugawa period. It united opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s concessions to Western powers. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> treat the movement as a force behind the overthrow of the shogunate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sonno-joi.jpg" alt="Sonnō jōi" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ippōsai Yoshifuji (Japanese, 1828–1887) (一鵬斎芳藤) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yokohama-Sumo-Wrestler-Defeating-a-Foreigner-1861-Ipposai-Yoshifuji.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sonnō jōi is the Japanese reading of the Chinese idiom zūn wáng rǎng yí, coined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a>, chancellor of Qi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>, in reference to the Zhou kings. By adhering to it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a> assembled the Chinese feudal lords against the threat of foreign peoples, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> praised Guan Zhong for preserving Chinese civilisation. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects of Confucius</a> the expression passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>, which read the Chunqiu with this concept. The 17th-century Confucian scholars Yamazaki Ansai and Yamaga Sokō wrote on the sanctity of the Imperial House and its superiority to the ruling houses of other nations. Takenouchi Shikibu's theory of absolute loyalty to the emperor implied that less loyalty was owed to the Tokugawa. The Mito scholar Aizawa Seishisai brought the term into modern Japanese in his Shinron in 1825.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sonnō jōi, meaning revere the emperor, expel the barbarians, was the rallying cry of a political movement of the 1850s and 1860s, the Bakumatsu period. Based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> and Japanese nativism, it sought to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and restore the power of the emperor. As foreign ships entered Japanese waters more often from the late 18th century, the policy of national seclusion came into question, and the jōi part of the slogan turned into a reaction against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa">Convention of Kanagawa</a> of 1854, signed under duress under the threat of Commodore Matthew C. Perry's black ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a> and other anti-Tokugawa leaders took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s powerlessness before the foreigners, despite the will of the imperial court, as proof that the shogunate must be replaced. The slogan became a battle cry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a> and Satsuma Province. Emperor Kōmei agreed with it, took an active part in state affairs and in March 1863 issued his Order to Expel Barbarians. The shogunate had no intention of enforcing it, but it inspired attacks on the shogunate and on foreigners, notably the killing of the trader Charles Lennox Richardson in the Namamugi Incident and the shelling of foreign shipping at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>.</p><p>This was the movement's high point. The Western powers demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and in 1864 four Western nations overran the defences at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a> and briefly occupied the region. The episode showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was no match for Western military powers and further weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, which allowed the rebel provinces to ally and overthrow it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. The slogan was never an actual government or rebel policy. After the restoration it was replaced by fukoku kyōhei, enrich the nation, strengthen the armies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sonnō jōi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>sonnō jōi</b> movement ('revere the emperor, expel the barbarians') gave opponents of the shogunate their ideology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mito School, Aizawa Seishisai’s Shinron (“New Theses”) of 1825 argued that <b>sonnō jōi</b>, “revere the Emperor, expel the barbarian”, would unite the people, with the Emperor revered as the symbol of the kokutai, the national polity.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
